## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · MSTest · .NET 10  
**Integration calibration applied:** Class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell #1)

**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 95)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means only one assertion branch ever runs. If the type changes unexpectedly, the test still passes via the other branch — hiding a regression.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_ReturnsStandardUser()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsNotInstanceOfType(loaded, typeof(PremiumUser));
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}
```
Split into two tests if premium-user retrieval also needs coverage.

**Risk if unfixed:** One code path is silently untested; regressions hide behind the branch that still passes.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell #4)

**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Problem:** No assertion. If `Insert` silently drops records, corrupts data, or becomes a no-op, this test still passes.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage metrics look healthy while zero behavior is actually verified.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test (Smell #3) *(downgraded from High — integration calibration)*

**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Problem:** Hard 3-second wait every run. Multiplied across CI runs this adds significant time, and a slow machine may still race.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of fixed sleep
    var notified = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(notified, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slow suite, flaky failures on loaded CI agents (Camara et al., SAST 2021 correlation with flakiness).

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

4 assertions, none with a message parameter. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to a line number but provides no semantic context.

**Fix — add messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis when failures occur in CI logs without IDE context.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-structured:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused arrange-act-assert, clear intent |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, negative assertion proves deletion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion is contextually obvious (3 items added → expect 3); not Magic Number |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Clean async pattern with proper await and assertion |

The fixture (`[TestInitialize]`) uses an in-memory SQLite database — this is **not** Mystery Guest because the resource is hermetic and deterministic. All fields are used by every test, so no General Fixture smell applies either.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 2 min | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 10 min | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into type-specific tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 15 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-with-timeout helper |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 5 min | Add message strings to multi-assert tests |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 7 tests are clean. The two High-severity findings (#1 and #2) represent real risk of hidden regressions and should be fixed first — both are quick wins.
